<title>ITALY: Reuters Asset Allocation Poll (Italy) - May 9.</title>
<headline>Reuters Asset Allocation Poll (Italy) - May 9.</headline>
<dateline>MILAN 1997-05-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Italian banks and fund managers have made a small shift into Italian equities this month in the expectation of a positive trend for the lira and the stock market, a Reuter Asset Allocation poll for May showed.</p>
<p>While most banks and fund managers had an almost unchanged investment outlook for May, the total weight on Italian equities rose to 24.4 percent from 22.6 percent in April.</p>
<p>One institution which declined to be named increased its weight in Italian equities to 40 percent from 20 percent in April.</p>
<p>&quot;This reflects the first signs of economic pickup in Germany which improve the profit and turnover outlook for Italian exporters. This should help the Italian bourse,&quot; an analyst at the bank said.</p>
<p>The analyst also noted the positive trend for the lira and the fact that the Italian bourse had dipped in April and now looked ready to test some new highs.</p>
<p>Caboto retained the same investment weight on Italian bonds (30 percent), but brought 50 percent of the weight which in April was on the short end of the curve to the middle of the curve.</p>
<p>&quot;This reflects the idea that the political risk in Italy is limited and from the beginning of May it might be interesting to start accumulating on the medium term part of the curve,&quot; Ilaria Bertizzolo, analyst at Caboto said.</p>
<p>Caboto also increased weighting on foreign bonds and equities to 20 percent in total from 10 percent previously, putting 10 percent on each sector. For foreign bonds it put half the weighting on UK Gilts and half on emerging markets.</p>
<p>&quot;This is because we see the possibility for Gilts to appreciate since Britain is now less far away from monetary union and this should cause British interest rates to converge with German ones in the medium term,&quot; Caboto said.</p>
<p>Reuters collected data on the distribution of a typical portfolio in lire from eight Italian banks and investment funds from May 5 to 7. The data in parenthesis refer to data in the April poll. The April averages are based on the allocation from 10 institutions, two of which did not take part in the May poll.  </p>
